Integration checklist — exact replacement patterns
If your existing code contains lines like:
phase_error = phi_stat - phi_rot  # in radians
T = k_t * I * np.cos(phase_error)
Replace with:
# option A: if you have scalar phases
T = scalar_signed_torque_from_phase(k_t, I, phi_stat, phi_rot)

# option B: if you have phasor components already (recommended)
u = np.array([I_stat_cos, I_stat_sin])
v = np.array([I_rot_cos, I_rot_sin])
T = signed_torque_proxy(k_t, 1.0, u, v) * (I_scale)   # careful: scale consistent with phasor magnitudes
If you use an objective inside an optimizer or a hill-climb that does not need signed torque:
obj = squared_torque_proxy(k_t, I, u, v)   # maximize this

MCU / fixed-point and performance notes
    • The inner-loop can avoid any sqrt if you operate on squared proxies — that’s ideal on MCU. Only compute sqrt() when you need signed amplitude to command a current magnitude (one sqrt per control frame is fine).
    • Keep the eps guard to avoid divide-by-zero.
    • If you use phasor ADCs that provide in-phase/quadrature samples (Icos, Isin), feed them directly as u/v.
    • For fixed-point, scale all floats by e.g. 2^20, compute using 64-bit integers for intermediates, then shift back. Use saturation clamps.

