Discussion & caveats (brief)
    • Proxy vs exact cos: mathematically the proxy is equivalent to cos^2 and recovers sign via D. In typical phasor scenarios the parity is excellent. Parity plot will quantify any tiny numerical mismatch from finite numeric rounding.
    • Hill-climb behavior: switching the objective to squared torque avoids local minima related to sign flips, but you must use sign(D) when commanding polarity.
    • MCU resource: if you already compute phasor components (Icos/Isin) you save a lot — the algebraic proxy is extremely cheap: a few multiplies and adds + a divide. If you need sign+mag you do one sqrt.
    • Numerical stability: watch very small Qu or Qv when phasors are nearly zero (e.g., motor off); code has a small eps guard, but you may want stronger gating: if Qu < threshold, treat as zero torque and skip optimization.